  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000753",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 6 candidates are direct matches on concept and endpoint (trait_reported/trait_efo are abdominal aortic aneurysm; phenotyping_reported is AAA, with PGS000753 explicitly prevalent AAA). The strongest PRS-comparable evidence in the pool is for PGS000753: it reports an effect size (OR per SD 1.37, 95% CI 1.30–1.44) with basic covariates (age, sex, PCs), which is more directly interpretable for PRS discrimination than full-model AUROC/C-index values. Competing models mainly report full-model discrimination (e.g., AUROC 0.868 for PGS001784; C-index 0.882 for PGS003973) without clear PRS-only AUROC, and domain guidance cautions these can be largely covariate-driven; PGS001784 does provide a covariates-regressed-out Nagelkerke R2=0.014661, but it comes from a portability/framework-style paper and the metric advantage over others is modest. Several other candidates have missing PRS-only metrics and/or unknown covariates, limiting confident ranking. Therefore a direct match exists, but the evidence is insufficiently decisive for a high-confidence single best choice."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001777","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All three candidates are direct matches to the target concept (trait_efo = alcoholic liver cirrhosis) with broadly aligned endpoints in phenotyping_reported. However, none reports clearly PRS-only discrimination (PGS-only AUROC/R²), so cross-model ranking must rely on endpoint fidelity plus the limited available performance/effect-size evidence.

PGS001777 is preferred because it is disease-focused on alcohol-related cirrhosis and provides the most directly PRS-informative signal among the set via a large risk gradient (Odds Ratio top 20% vs bottom 20% = 6.18, with AUROC 0.671 reported) despite using only 3 variants. PGS000704 reports a higher full-model C-index (0.711) but this is explicitly a full-model metric with demographic covariates and lacks PRS-only metrics; its per-SD HR=1.18 suggests only modest standalone PRS signal. PGS004913 reports an R²=0.029 and OR top vs bottom deciles=4.4, but the R² is not explicitly PRS-only and the evidence package is limited (no classification metrics shown).

Main limitation: the available metrics are not clearly comparable PRS-only measures across candidates (full-model AUROC/C-index and unspecified R²), and covariates are unknown for PGS001777, so the selection is supported but not definitive."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS005252","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 returned candidates, only four are direct matches to the target disease concept (aortic stenosis): PGS005252 and PGS005254 (trait_reported/trait_efo = aortic stenosis) plus PGS005255/PGS005256 (aortic valve function measures but phenotyping_reported still states incident aortic stenosis). The remaining four models are for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and are not direct matches.

PGS005252 is best supported within the direct-match set because it has clear endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: incident aortic stenosis cases; trait_efo: aortic stenosis), uses a genome-wide shrinkage method (LDPred2) with a large variant set (1,119,377), and provides both a time-to-event effect size (HR=1.92) and a reported discrimination metric (C-index=0.87) in European evaluation. Competing direct-match candidates (PGS005254/PGS005255/PGS005256) report HRs (1.64 and 1.53) but provide no discrimination metrics in the visible metadata, making them less well-supported on comparable performance evidence.

Main limitation: the reported C-index for PGS005252 is a full-model metric with many clinical covariates (e.g., diabetes, hypertension, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure), so it cannot be interpreted as PRS-only performance; no PRS-only AUROC/R² is provided for any aortic stenosis candidate here, which lowers certainty and keeps confidence at Moderate."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001263",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches in the sense that their trait_efo includes atrial flutter, but only PGS001263 has phenotype alignment that is specific to the target (phenotyping_reported and trait_reported both “Atrial flutter”), whereas PGS001339/PGS001841/PGS002050 use the broader combined endpoint “Atrial fibrillation and flutter” (including a time-to-event variant for PGS001339). PGS001263 also provides explicit PRS-comparable metrics (PGS AUROC (no covariates)=0.61716; PGS R2 (no covariates)=0.02517) and these are below the provided heritability ceiling (h2_obs=0.0157; h2_liability=0.1443), so they are not obviously inconsistent. However, the strongest-looking AUROC shown for PGS001263 (0.78179) is a full-model metric with covariates and should not be treated as PRS-only evidence; and the alternative models from the portability paper (PGS001841/PGS002050) lack AUC/R2 and only report partial-r, making cross-model performance comparison incomplete. Given the framework-study origin of PGS001263 and limited comparable performance evidence across all candidates, the selection is direct but not high-certainty."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches by trait_reported/trait_efo, but PGS000327 has substantially better endpoint fidelity: phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Autism spectrum disorder” (a direct clinical disease endpoint), whereas PGS002790’s phenotyping_reported is “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)”, creating an endpoint mismatch despite the surface ASD label. PGS000327 also provides supportive PRS-quality evidence (OR per SD 1.33 with CI 1.30–1.36 and an R² metric reported as 0.0245) with only basic covariates (genetic PCs, genotyping wave) and a disease-focused publication context (Nat Genet ASD GWAS). However, confidence is limited because neither model reports clearly PRS-only discrimination metrics (e.g., PGS-only AUROC or covariates-regressed-out R²), and the reported R² fields may reflect full-model/partial metrics rather than strictly PRS-comparable performance."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002786",
  "confidence": "Low",
  "rationale": "All three candidates are direct matches to the target umbrella trait (bipolar disorder spectrum) via trait_reported/trait_efo, but endpoint fidelity is unclear/possibly misaligned because phenotyping_reported fields are non-diagnostic (cognitive/behavioral measures) rather than bipolar disorder case-control/diagnosis endpoints. Performance evidence is limited and not clearly PRS-comparable: only small full_model_r2/partial R² values are shown (no PGS-only AUROC/R², no effect sizes), and covariates are basic (age, PCs1-3) across all models, offering little differentiation. Given indistinguishable study family/method (all SDPR, EUR-only GWAS/EVAL) and similar validation sizes, PGS002786 is selected only because it is the broadest concept match (bipolar disorder rather than subtype I/II), but this is a marginal advantage and does not resolve the endpoint-fidelity concern."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001174",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 6 candidates are direct matches to cholelithiasis at the concept level (either exact cholelithiasis/K80 or a closely related composite endpoint including cholelithiasis). PGS001174 is preferred because it is the only model in the set with clearly reported PRS-comparable performance metrics, including PGS-only AUROC (0.62342) and PGS-only R2 (0.02808) plus incremental AUROC (0.04311) under basic covariates, and these PRS-only metrics are plausibly below the provided heritability ceiling (h2_obs=0.0214; h2_liability=0.1372), supporting interpretability. Main limitation: PGS001174 is from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework paper and uses a time-to-event phenotype, both of which are warning signals for external generalization; meanwhile several newer exact-endpoint K80/cholelithiasis models (PGS004476/PGS004546/PGS005150) are disease-labeled but lack comparable AUC/R2 evidence (only modest OR/beta reported and covariates unknown for two), preventing a high-confidence, clearly best-supported choice."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002034",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Two direct-match candidates are present (PGS001826, PGS002034), and both align on the same endpoint fields: trait_efo matches the target trait and phenotyping_reported/trait_reported are identical (“Congenital deficiency of other clotting factors (including factor VII)”). Both come from the same UKB portability publication, use similar basic covariates, have the same training cohort context and validation sample size, and report essentially identical European validation partial correlation (0.0562 vs 0.0563 with overlapping CIs), so performance evidence does not clearly separate them. Given the domain guidance to use structural signals when PRS-only AUC/R2 are absent and candidates are otherwise near-clones, PGS002034 is mildly preferred because it uses LDpred2 (genome-wide shrinkage) with far more variants (32,552 vs 45), which is generally a stronger single-score construction for polygenic signal capture. The main limitation is that both models are from a portability/framework-style UKB study and only provide a modest partial-r metric (no PRS-only AUC/R2 or effect sizes), making the direct-match recommendation less strongly supported overall."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000922",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches by concept label, but two (PGS001872, PGS002084) have trait_efo=\"skin sarcoidosis\", which is a subtype mismatch relative to the target \"sarcoidosis\" and lowers endpoint fidelity despite phenotyping_reported being generic \"Sarcoidosis\". The remaining two (PGS000922, PGS000923) have coherent disease-level alignment (trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported all sarcoidosis) and provide PRS-comparable metrics including PGS-only AUROC and PGS-only R2 with only basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). Between them, PGS000922 is preferred because it targets the generic disease endpoint (not time-to-event), while performance is very similar (PGS-only AUROC 0.64282; PGS-only R2 0.01741) and both pass the heritability sanity check (PGS-only R2 well below h2_liability=0.1070). However, confidence is not high because both top candidates come from a pan-trait UK Biobank framework paper (\"across 813 traits\"), which domain guidance flags as often less externally robust, and there is no clearly disease-focused multi-cohort alternative in the provided set."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005219",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates are present for sleep apnea, including multiple models labeled \"Sleep apnea\" (PGS003204–PGS003218) and several labeled \"Obstructive sleep apnea\" (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge states obstructive sleep apnea is the dominant clinical form of sleep apnea, so OSA models are acceptable direct matches.\n\nAmong the OSA candidates, PGS005219 (Obstructive sleep apnea, AdjustedBMI) has the strongest visible PRS-strength signal via effect size (OR=1.98, 95% CI 1.74–2.24) and uses a multi-score aggregation-style method (weighted PRS summation of PRS-CS components), which domain knowledge flags as a positive structural signal. It also reports a higher full-model AUROC (0.79) than the ExPRSweb sleep-apnea framework cluster (full-model AUROC ~0.49–0.53).\n\nMain limitation: the reported AUROC values are full-model (not PGS-only), and PGS005219 includes BMI as a covariate while also being BMI-adjusted in the phenotype naming, making cross-model comparability to the generic \"Sleep apnea\" endpoints uncertain from the provided metadata. Additionally, several ExPRSweb models form a coherent direct-match cluster but appear framework-derived and show near-null discrimination (AUROC ~0.5), leaving the overall direct-match evidence set mixed rather than clearly high-quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002075",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All five candidates are direct matches to the target concept urolithiasis via either explicit trait_efo=urolithiasis (PGS001864, PGS002075) or closely aligned kidney/ureter calculus/nephrolithiasis endpoints (PGS001250, PGS004493, PGS004563). Among models with PRS-comparable performance evidence, PGS002075 reports the strongest available PRS-comparable metric (partial correlation 0.0385, CI 0.0246–0.0524) versus PGS001864 (partial-r 0.0323), with the same reported phenotype (“Urinary calculus”), covariates, training cohort (UKB), and evaluation ancestry distribution, making it the best-supported choice within that coherent sibling set. PGS001250 provides explicit PGS-only AUROC/R2 but comes from a pan-trait sparse UKB framework paper and uses a time-to-event endpoint, which domain guidance flags as a generalization risk despite having more complete metrics. The 2024 nephrolithiasis models (PGS004493/PGS004563) provide only modest ORs (~1.23–1.28) and have unknown covariates with no AUROC/R2, limiting comparability. Overall confidence is reduced because key discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R2) are missing for the preferred model family and several candidates have incomplete or non-comparable performance reporting."
}
